module lib/polar
  Newton-Schulz iteration: a matrix polynomial that moves every singular value
  of a matrix toward one without changing its singular vectors, and without
  computing an SVD. Run to convergence with the classical coefficients it
  gives the polar factor, the closest matrix with orthonormal rows; run for
  five rounds with Muon's coefficients it gives the direction the Muon
  optimizer steps along. The post at the top of lib/polar.viv compares the two.

frob  @58a88086
  what   The Frobenius norm of a matrix: the square root of the sum of the
         squares of its entries.
  A      the matrix

ns_poly  @40bd191f
  what  What one step does to a single singular value. The matrix iteration is this
        polynomial applied to each of them at once, which is the whole reason the
        coefficients can be reasoned about at all.

ns_step  @9e1bb87d
  what  One quintic Newton-Schulz step on an `m` by `n` matrix with `m <= n`. `X X^T` is
        the smaller of the two products, which is why Muon transposes a tall matrix
        before starting.
  why   With `X = U S V^T`, the step gives `U p(S) V^T`: the singular vectors stay,
        and each singular value s becomes `p(s) = a s + b s^3 + c s^5`.
  X     the current iterate, `m` by `n`
  a     the coefficient of `X`
  b     the coefficient of `X X^T X`
  c     the coefficient of `(X X^T)^2 X`
  S     `X X^T`, `m` by `m`

  $$ X \gets a X + b\, (X X^\top) X + c\, (X X^\top)^2 X $$

ns_normalize  @00720aa4
  what  Divide by the Frobenius norm, which puts every singular value in `(0, 1]`
        because the Frobenius norm is at least the largest of them. No eigenvalue
        computation is needed to start.
  A     the matrix
  f     its Frobenius norm; a zero matrix is returned as it is

newton_schulz  @6a65b959
  what   `steps` rounds of the iteration with the coefficients given, from the
         matrix divided by its Frobenius norm.
  A      the matrix, `m` by `n` with `m <= n`
  steps  how many rounds
  a      the coefficient of `X`
  b      the coefficient of `X X^T X`
  c      the coefficient of `(X X^T)^2 X`
  X      the iterate
  k      the round

polar_factor  @18d9f856
  what  The polar factor of `A`: the closest matrix with orthonormal rows, by the
        classical iteration run long enough to have converged.

muon_direction  @b65264ef
  what  The direction Muon would step in, given a momentum matrix: five rounds of the
        tuned polynomial. Not orthonormal, and not meant to be.
  why   What the step needs is that no direction of the matrix gets a much larger
        step than another: singular values of about the same size, not exactly
        one. Muon's coefficients get there in fewer rounds than the classical ones.
  G     the momentum matrix, `m` by `n` with `m <= n`

polar_h  @f1fe57d6
  what  The symmetric positive semidefinite half of the decomposition, `H = U^T A`, so
        that `A = U H`.

singular_values  @132aae47
  what  The singular values of an `m` by `n` matrix with `m <= n`, descending, from
        `lib/svd`. Used to check what the iteration did, never inside it.

row_orthonormality  @fffd0e32
  what  How far the rows of `U` are from orthonormal: the largest entry of `U U^T - I`.

polar_test_matrix  @34b18761
  what  A matrix with the singular values asked for, `m` by `n` with `m` the number of
        values and `m <= n`, built from two orthogonal factors that have nothing to do
        with the axes.
